---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '~/components/common/JsonLd.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';

const metadata = {
  title: `SUP Hull Design — Rocker, Rails, and Displacement vs Planing Hulls | ${SITE.name}`,
  description:
    'Technical guide to inflatable SUP hull design: rocker profiles, rail shapes, displacement vs planing hulls. How shape affects stability, speed, tracking, and handling.',
  ignoreTitleTemplate: true,
};
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'SUP Hull Design Guide',
  description: metadata.description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name },
  datePublished: '2026-07-27',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <JsonLd data={jsonLd} />
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-sky-600">{SITE.name}</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><a
        href="/research"
        class="hover:text-sky-600">Research</a
      ><span class="mx-2">/</span><span class="text-gray-900">SUP Hull Design</span>
    </nav>
    <div class="mb-4">
      <span
        class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-600 bg-sky-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full"
        >Engineering</span
      ><span class="ml-3 text-sm text-gray-400">9 min read</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">SUP Hull Design Guide</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 mb-12 leading-relaxed">
      Every dimension of a SUP hull — rocker, rail shape, entry line, and bottom contour — is engineered for a specific
      performance profile. Understanding hull design helps you match a board to your paddling style.
    </p>
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-p:text-gray-600">
      <h2>Rocker Profile</h2>
      <p>
        Rocker is the curvature of the board from nose to tail when viewed from the side. More rocker (higher nose
        curve) helps the board ride over chop and waves without pearling (nose diving). Less rocker (flatter profile)
        maximizes waterline length for better glide and tracking. All-around SUPs use moderate rocker — enough nose lift
        for chop management without sacrificing too much waterline. Dedicated touring boards minimize rocker for maximum
        glide efficiency. Whitewater boards use significant nose rocker to prevent pearling in steep river drops.
      </p>
      <h2>Rail Shape</h2>
      <p>
        The rail (edge of the board) significantly affects stability and turning. Hard rails (sharper edge) bite into
        the water for better tracking but can catch in crosswind conditions. Soft rails (rounded edge) release water
        more easily for smoother carving but provide less edge hold. Tapered rails (thicker at the center, thinner at
        the edge) reduce drag while maintaining volume. Inflatable SUP rails are naturally softer than hard boards,
        which is one reason inflatables feel more stable to beginners but less responsive to advanced paddlers.
      </p>
      <h2>Displacement vs Planing Hull</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Displacement hulls</strong> have a pointed bow that parts the water, creating a clean entry and efficient
        forward motion. The hull pushes water aside (displaces it) rather than riding on top. Displacement hulls excel at
        straight-line speed and tracking in flat to moderate chop. They are standard on touring and racing SUPs.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Planing hulls</strong> have a flatter, wider bow that rides on top of the water rather than pushing through
        it. They are more stable at rest and plane onto the surface at speed for reduced drag. Planing hulls turn more easily
        and are preferred for surfing, whitewater, and all-around recreational use. Most all-around SUPs use a hybrid design
        with a slight displacement entry transitioning to a planing tail.
      </p>
      <h2>Bottom Contour</h2>
      <p>
        Flat bottom: maximum primary stability, best for beginners and yoga. V-bottom (single concave): channels water
        for improved tracking, common on touring boards. Double concave: reduces drag at speed, channels water to the
        fins for enhanced hold. Inflatable SUP construction limits bottom contour complexity compared to hard boards
        because the PVC must maintain a smooth inflation shape, but advanced manufacturers use strategic reinforcement
        panels to create subtle bottom contours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
